  [1mNow the question that decides whether this feature is interesting or trivial.[0m In a single-user app, you are the only
  one who changes anything — so a literal reading of "notify me when tasks change" means notifying you about edits you
  just made, which is noise. The feature only earns its place if "change" means something you [3mdidn't[0m personally just do.
  There are three candidates, and they differ in how much machinery they need:

  - [1mTime crossing a threshold[0m — a task becomes due soon, or goes overdue. Nothing changed the task; the clock moved.
    Needs a timer/scheduler and a notion of "already fired" so it doesn't re-alert on every page load. This is the one
    that makes a single-user notification system genuinely useful.
  - [1mEdits you made, as an audit trail[0m — a visible history of what changed and when. No scheduler, no unread semantics
    really; it's a log view, not notifications. Nearly free once the change log exists.
  - [1mChanges from outside this tab[0m — another device, another browser tab, an import. Real "something happened while you
    weren't looking" semantics, but it needs sync, which is the backend we just deferred.

  [1mMy recommendation: time-based thresholds as the core, with the change log surfaced as a secondary activity view.[0m That
  gives you a notification system that actually tells you something you didn't know, keeps the scheduler small (one
  timer, one fired-set), and the activity view comes almost free since we're building the log anyway. Cross-device is
  the right answer [3mafter[0m a server exists — building sync now would reverse the decision you just made.
